// D-EFFTREE1: the ten D-EFF4/5 effect names (`Net`, `FS`, `DB`, …) are tree
// ROOTS. A leaf under one — `FS.Read`, `FS.Write` — is expressible on a
// signature, and ancestor matching is subsumption: a `=[FS]=>` bound accepts
// any `FS.*` callee. This is the same rule D-TAG1 already taught for nested
// tag/variant groups — learned once, applied to effects too.
//
// `load` is scoped to the precise leaf `FS.Read`; a reviewer can see at a
// glance it can never delete anything. `archive` is scoped to `FS.Write`.
// `boot` declares the ROOT `FS` (plus `IO` for its own `print` calls) — `FS`
// subsumes both the `FS.Read` and `FS.Write` leaves reached through `load`/
// `archive`, exactly as `=[FS]=>` already covered a bare `FS` callee before
// this decision (no migration break for existing flat code).
fn load(path: String) =[FS.Read]=> String = "contents of {path}"

fn archive(path: String) =[FS.Write]=> String = "archived {path}"

fn boot() =[FS, IO]=> {
    print(load("app.conf"))
    print(archive("out.tar"))
}

fn run() { boot() }
